        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Third Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan III)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>Predator-and-prey imagery (paired with seed 1:3 &#x27;I will grasp the sons of Adam like the chicks of a bird&#x27;): the Devil&#x27;s MODUS-OPERANDI against humanity is described in raptor-and-snare vocabulary. Predator-imagery for the Devil parallels: 1 PETER 5:8 (&#x27;the devil, as a roaring lion, walketh about, seeking whom he may devour&#x27;); PSALM 91:13 (read patristically as Christ-defeats-the-lion-and-the-dragon); PSALM 124:7 (&#x27;our soul is escaped as a bird out of the snare of the fowlers&#x27;); MATTHEW 13:4 + 19 (Satan as the birds-that-eat-the-seed in the Sower parable&#x27;s interpretation). Tewahedo iconography preserves the Satan-as-lion + Satan-as-raptor typology in martyr-saint hagiographic art. The verse functions as ANTHROPOLOGICAL-PROTOLOGY: humans are FRAGILE-VULNERABLE prey before divine-protection intervenes — the Christological-pneumatological deliverance-doctrine that the rest of the chapter develops.</p></aside>',
