        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Third Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan III)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>CHAPTER-CLOSING TRANSITION — closes the chapter&#x27;s cosmological-introduction sequence. The closing verses likely include a brief doxological-pause before Ch 2&#x27;s God-response opens. The intra-chapter-closing pattern parallels the standard EOTC homiletic-anthology genre: chapters end with miniature-doxologies preparing the next thematic development (cf. the inner-doxologies of LXX Sirach + Wisdom of Solomon + 4 Maccabees). Tewahedo lectionary tradition preserves the chapter-by-chapter homiletic-reading structure for Säʿatat (Liturgy of the Hours). The verse closes the Devil&#x27;s-hubris-and-fall narrative — the longest sustained-Devil-first-person-speech section in EOTC canonical literature — and prepares the transition to the divine-response architecture of Ch 2. The Devil-speaks pattern is suspended; the homiletic-mode shifts to God-speaks for the chapter-break.</p></aside>',
