        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Third Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan III)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>EXPANSION of Job-allusion (paired with seed 2:1 &#x27;But you, who could not deceive my servant JOB...&#x27;): the chapter develops Job&#x27;s patient-suffering as PARADIGM for human-resistance-to-Devil&#x27;s-deception. The Job-as-paradigm reading parallels: JAMES 5:11 (&#x27;Ye have heard of the patience of Job, and have seen the end of the Lord; that the Lord is very pitiful, and of tender mercy&#x27;); 1 CLEMENT 17 (Job alongside Abraham + Moses as faith-exemplars); BARUCH 3:30 (Job in the wisdom-tradition catalog); TESTAMENT OF JOB (an early-Jewish-Christian Job-haggadah from 1st c. BCE-CE; Schaller 1979). Patristic Job-as-Christ-typology readings: AMBROSE De Interpellatione Iob et David; CHRYSOSTOM Commentary on Job; GREGORY THE GREAT Moralia in Iob (the foundational Latin Job-commentary). Meqabyan&#x27;s distinctive contribution: the Job-paradigm is DELIVERED VIA THE DEVIL&#x27;S DEFEAT — Job&#x27;s victory is presented as evidence-against-the-Devil&#x27;s-claim that humans can be deceived. Theological move: prosopopoeia of the Devil-as-defeated-petitioner.</p></aside>',
