        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Third Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan III)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>Theme of DIVINE-JUSTICE distinct from divine-permission: the verse develops the theological distinction between God permitting Satan-trial (Job 1:12 + 2:6) vs God authorizing Satan-defeat (Job 42:7-8 + 42:10). The permission-vs-defeat distinction parallels patristic theodicy: AUGUSTINE City of God 11.17 (God permits-but-does-not-author evil); JOHN OF DAMASCUS De Fide Orthodoxa 4.19-20 (the distinction between God&#x27;s antecedent and consequent wills); MAXIMUS THE CONFESSOR Quaestiones ad Thalassium 1 (God permits Satan&#x27;s rebellion to maximize-cosmic-good via greater-redemption); GREGORY NAZIANZEN Oration 31.15. Meqabyan&#x27;s distinctive contribution: the permission-vs-defeat theological framework is delivered VIA THE DEVIL&#x27;S RECOGNITION of his own defeat — the structural irony of the Devil acknowledging God&#x27;s-victory. Tewahedo theological reception reads 2:9 as the canonical proof-text for distinguishing PERMITTED-SUFFERING from authored-injustice.</p></aside>',
