        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Third Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan III)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>PRE-FALL-NAME debate (vv. 3-5 elaboration of seed 4:5 &#x27;You became proud; YOU WERE CALLED THE DEVIL; YOUR ARMIES WERE CALLED DEMONS&#x27;): the verse implies a debate about the PRE-FALL NAME. The patristic tradition gives Lucifer (Latin lucifer = &#x27;light-bearer&#x27;; cf. TERTULLIAN Adversus Marcionem 5.11 + ORIGEN Hom on Ezekiel 13.2 + AUGUSTINE Confessions 13.8 readings of Isa 14:12); the Greek tradition: φωσφόρος (phosphoros, dawn-bearer); the Syriac tradition: ܒܪ ܫܚܪܐ (Bar-Shahre, son-of-the-morning). The Geʽez tradition preserves both Lucifer-equivalents (አኹ ለፈጂር, akhu le-fajir, &#x27;brother of the dawn&#x27;) and a distinctive ሌዊያታን (Levyatan, Leviathan as primordial-rebel). Meqabyan&#x27;s distinctive contribution: the pre-fall-name is left UNDETERMINED — the etymological-fall-doctrine is the name&#x27;s ARRIVAL via fall, not the displacement-of-a-known-pre-fall-name. Tewahedo angelology preserves this ambiguity intentionally.</p></aside>',
