        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Third Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan III)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>FOUR-ELEMENTS ADAMIC ANTHROPOLOGY (paralleling 2 Mq 14:19 four-elements-resurrection): the verse develops the Empedoclean/Galenic four-elements (earth + water + fire + wind) reapplied to Adam&#x27;s creation. Direct parallels: GENESIS 2:7 (&#x27;God formed man of the dust of the ground, and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life&#x27;); WISDOM 7:1-3 (the elemental-composition of human-bodies); 2 ENOCH 30:8-9 (the seven-elements-of-Adam — Slavonic Enoch&#x27;s expanded-variation); EPHRAEM THE SYRIAN Carmina Nisibena 46.6-9 (Adam&#x27;s-body composed of dust + Spirit + water + fire — Syriac anthropology source for Tewahedo reception); SEVERUS OF ANTIOCH Cathedral Homilies 21 (Coptic-Syriac elemental-anthropology). The four-elements doctrine is the EMPEDOCLEAN/GALENIC mediated to Ethiopian Christianity via Syriac and Coptic patristic literature. Meqabyan&#x27;s distinctive contribution: the SAME four-elements that constitute Adam at creation are reconstituted at resurrection (2 Mq 14:19 explicit; 3 Mq 4:18 anthropological-creational anchor).</p></aside>',
