        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Third Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan III)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>TRANSITION TO COMPLETE-REPENTANCE rubric (vv. 30-33, preparing seed 4:34 &#x27;complete repentance&#x27; definition): the verse likely establishes the four-step-rubric architecture. The four-step rubric (sin + confession + weeping + non-return) parallels: PATRISTIC PENITENTIAL TRADITION more broadly — TERTULLIAN De Paenitentia (the foundational Latin-Christian penitential treatise); JOHN CHRYSOSTOM Homilies on Repentance (the foundational Greek-Christian treatment); GREGORY OF NYSSA Letter 4 (the canonical penitential-rubric); BASIL Letter 217 (the four-stage rubric for major sins). The Tewahedo Fetha Nagast (Law of Kings, 13th c.) codifies the four-step rubric directly drawing on this Meqabyan source-text. The verse-position likely establishes the THEOLOGICAL ARCHITECTURE for the rubric: WHY four steps + WHAT each step does + HOW the rubric integrates with the Devil-cannot-repent-because-pride-forbids-confession contrast at seed 4:34.</p></aside>',
